3 DISTINCT falsifiers                            3   []
3 EMPTY falsifiers                               3   []      <- no collapse
3 MISSING falsifier key                          3   []      <- no collapse
mixed: 2 same, 2 empty                           3   [['C0','C1']]
whitespace-only difference                       1   [['C0','C1']]   <- .strip()
internal whitespace differs                      2   []
```

Normalisation is `.strip()` only, so internally-differing whitespace makes
separate groups. That **over-counts distinct defects**, which is the safe
direction for an alarm whose sentence is "these N items are really M defects" —
it can only under-claim duplication, never invent it. The commit also notes it
groups from the **registry**, not the evidence dict, because the bundle carries
only a `falsifier_present` boolean — and says a behavioural test caught the
earlier keying error. That is the right lesson learned in the right place.

**`routing_history`: correct, unconditional, and never yet executed.** The write
at `:4977` sits immediately after every `route()` call, gated by nothing but a
telemetry `try/except`. It will fire. But:

```
archived runs with routing_history on any entry : 0
archived alarms carrying distinct_defects       : 0  (3 alarms, all pre-commit)
```

No run has happened since the commit, so **the claim "routing_history is written
for the first time" is a claim about code, not about evidence.** That is exactly
the age-control case `latent_control_audit`'s own docstring warns about — a key
