### What follows: yes, wire the adjudication — but not as the pre-registration writes it

The pre-registration's remedy is that **the rubric governs where the two
disagree, and the adjudication is logged.** Wire the *logging* immediately. Be
careful about the *governing*.

κ = −0.023 means these two instruments are not a strong and a weak measurement of
one quantity. They are **two different quantities wearing one name.** The numeric
is a model-authored scalar; the rubric is a consequence test. Declaring the
rubric the winner of a disagreement is defensible — it is the falsifiable one,
and it is the one the pre-registration ratified — but it should be recorded as a
**replacement of the instrument**, not as an *adjudication* between two readings.
Calling it adjudication implies the numeric retains residual authority, and at
κ = −0.023 it has none to retain.

What I would build, smallest first:

1. **`severity_rubric` as a recorded field beside `severity`, deciding nothing.**
   One round of real runs where both are written and neither gates. This costs
   nothing and is the only way to get a *prospective* disagreement sample; every
   number above is retrospective.
2. **An adjudication log keyed on the finding**, recording numeric, rubric,
   clause fired, and which governed. Not a summary count — the per-item record,
   because that is what makes clause 3's 67-item pile re-auditable.
3. **Only then**, flip the gate to the rubric, behind the same declare-the-mode
   discipline as Q2. The gate change is one line; the evidence that it is safe is
   the round in step 1.

This respects the constraint that matters: **the rubric is not a vote either.**
It is five consequence clauses evaluated against a finding, and a clause either
fires or it does not. That is the same shape as a falsifier, which is why it is
the right instrument to promote — and it is why it must be logged per-item, so a
human can see which clause did the work.

