### Q3 — metering: the meter that exists records nothing on the path that matters

`bench/dispatch_meter.py` is careful work. It refuses to estimate, refuses to
price, refuses to gate, installs nothing in simulated mode, and names its
uncovered route (`codex_exec`) instead of papering over it. All correct, and all
the right instincts.

**It is also broken, and its 9 passing tests cannot see it.**

The module patches two SDK primitives *in the calling process*. But
`runner_core.dispatch()` makes the model call inside `mp.Process`
(`runner_core.py:1408`), and this platform's start method is **spawn** — the
child is a fresh interpreter that re-imports the SDK **unpatched**. I proved it
rather than argued it, with a fake primitive of the same shape:

```
start method: spawn
rows after IN-PROCESS call (decomposed_dispatch leaves)  1
rows after CHILD-PROCESS call (the PRIMARY path)         0   <-- every ordinary dispatch
```

A meter armed only in the parent reports **zero for every ordinary dispatch**
while passing every test it has. That is the same defect its own docstring
convicts `cost_ledger` of — a module and its tests shipped, the wiring never
written — one layer further in. It is also *not currently imported by anything
outside its own test file*, so the wiring gap is literal as well.

**The smallest honest implementation, and the fix-measurement.** Two edits at the
ends of a channel that already exists — arm inside the child, and carry the rows
home on the queue that already carries the response:

```
_dispatch_worker:  arm(simulated=...) at entry
                   result_queue.put(("ok", response, drain()))
dispatch:          status, response, rows = ...; note_rows(rows)
```

Only provider-reported integers cross the boundary; the response object never
does. I added `note_rows()` to `bench/dispatch_meter.py` and measured it:

```
BEFORE  rows after a CHILD-PROCESS call: 0
AFTER   rows after a CHILD-PROCESS call: 1
        provider usage recovered: {'input_tokens': 10, 'output_tokens': 5, 'total_tokens': 15}
```

Pinned by `bench/tests/test_dispatch_meter_crosses_the_process_boundary_2026-09-02.py`
— 8 tests, behaviour across a real spawned process, not source-substring greps.
Both plausible wrong implementations die:

| mutant | result |
|---|---|
| gate `note_rows` on the parent's `_ARMED` | **KILLED** (3 failed) |
| drop the `isinstance(dict)` filter | **KILLED** (2 failed) |

`note_rows` is deliberately **not** gated on `_ARMED`: the parent makes no SDK
calls and may never arm, and discarding the child's real measurements on that
basis is exactly the silent-zero failure the module exists to prevent.

**What it should record so a future allocator can use it.** The current row —
`{primitive, model, provider_usage, unmetered_reason, ts}` — is right on
provenance and insufficient for allocation. An allocator asks *"what did this
seat buy me?"*, which needs the spend joined to the **outcome**. Add three keys,
all already in scope at the dispatch site:

- `run_id` and `round` — without these, spend cannot be attributed to a round,
  and the marginal cost of round *k* is the whole question convergence asks.
- `seat_label` **and** `model_identity` — the two are different in exactly the
  single-model mode Q2 is about, and an allocator that cannot tell six seats from
  six models will price a one-model panel as if it were six.
- `purpose` — one of `finding|falsifier|routing_rung|verification`. This is the
  key that makes the Q2 table costable: "rung 2 yields 0.89pp" is only a decision
  once you know rung 2's price.

That is the smallest set that turns a meter into an allocator input. Everything
else — pricing, caps, per-vendor rates — is a founder-controlled table on top,
and should stay out of the meter, as the module already argues.

