commits that did not survive are the ones that state a mechanism without a
denominator.

This is also the cheapest possible change, because it is **the project's own
founding rule turned on its own commit messages.** Tools decide, not votes —
including when the vote is your own confidence about your own patch. A commit
message is a claim; CDSFL's whole thesis is that a claim without an executable
witness is worth less than an admission of ignorance.

**A second-order note, offered because it is the thing most likely to bite next.**
240 source-substring assertions live across 82 of 218 test files. Those tests
cannot fail when behaviour changes, only when *text* changes — so they are
strongest exactly where you are weakest, which is distinguishing "the code says
it does X" from "the code does X". I would not rewrite them all. I would add one
rule: **a test for a guard must construct an input that reaches the guard.** The
absorb guard has thirteen tests and no input.

