#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Cross-model co-discovery: the edge the identity rule already computes and throws away.

THE DEFECT, MEASURED
--------------------
`record_codiscovery` (2026-08-23) records that a second model raised a defect
the registry already holds. It fires from exactly one place: the immune
pipeline's NK-cell triage, via `is_duplicate`/`duplicate_of`. Measured over the
archive on 2026-09-02:

    real runs PREDATING the feature   0 / 2247 findings   (the field did not exist)
    real runs AFTER the feature       2 /   20 findings   10.0%
    simulated runs after the feature 91 /  253 findings   36.0%

So the headline "2 of 2050 = 0.10%" is a CORPUS artefact, not a detector
failure: 2247 of those 2267 findings come from runs where no code could have
written the field. The honest statement is not "co-discovery is never detected"
but "co-discovery has been OBSERVABLE on 20 real findings".

THE STRUCTURAL HOLE, which is separate and is what this closes
--------------------------------------------------------------
`convergence_location.identity_decision` is the project's own calibrated
identity rule, it is ALREADY model-agnostic, and the runner already runs it on
every critical finding for the novelty count. When it returns `signature_merge`
or `same_computed_outcome` it has just decided that this finding and some prior
finding are ONE DEFECT -- and it returns a boolean and discards WHICH prior.
The edge is computed on every run and written nowhere. That is why the archive
cannot be re-partitioned: not because the schema keys on `source_model`, but
because the identity edges were never persisted.

This module recovers those edges, offline, from archived registries, using the
project's own primitives at their own calibrated thresholds. Nothing here is a
second identity rule; every tier calls into `convergence_location`.

WHY THIS IS NOT A VOTE
----------------------
The output is an EDGE SET and a COUNT. It is recorded, never decisive:

  * it does not change any finding's status, severity, verified, or
    falsifier_verdict;
  * it does not merge, delete, suppress or renumber any canonical entry;
  * it does not touch novel_this_round, gamma, or any convergence input;
  * it does not up-weight a finding for having been raised twice.

Two models describing one defect is evidence about COVERAGE -- how much of the
defect space a second seat re-finds -- and nothing else. Truth still comes only
from a falsifier that fires on the artefact and stays quiet on the corrected
copy.

WHY THE FALSIFIER BODY CANNOT BE THE PRIMARY RULE HERE
------------------------------------------------------
Commit 976b6b5 made "a different falsifier is a different defect" the primary
discriminator for ID-REUSE absorption, and that is correct there, because that
site is keyed lookup_alias(f.model_id, f.finding_id) -- SAME MODEL, both sides.
Inverting it across models does not work, and the archive says so exactly:
across the 477 real archived findings that carry falsifier code, the number of
byte-identical falsifier bodies shared by two DIFFERENT models is ZERO. Real
vendors do not write the same code for the same defect. Falsifier identity is
kept as tier 0 -- when it fires it is decisive and free -- and reported
separately, with its measured yield, so nobody mistakes its silence for the
absence of co-discovery.

CALIBRATED, NOT ASSERTED -- and the first build of this was WRONG
------------------------------------------------------------------
The first run returned 56.10% and I nearly reported it. Sampling eight linked
pairs at random showed several were two models' PROTOCOL CHATTER, and several
more were two DIFFERENT defects in one function -- which is the exact failure
fable refuted in the absorb guard at 976b6b5: a STEM signature is built from
LOCATION tokens, so two findings about one function collide by construction.
The rule that was too loose for absorption is too loose for co-discovery, for
the same reason, and I walked into it from the other side.

So the tiers were calibrated against a BLIND TWO-READER adjudication of 50
cross-model pairs (experimental_notes/data/codiscovery_adjudication_2026-09-02
.json). Readers saw only the two descriptions -- no model labels, no tier, no
linker verdict -- and the pack mixed linked pairs with same-location UNLINKED
controls so the mix could not be inferred. Inter-reader agreement 47/50 = 94.0%,
Cohen kappa 0.896 [0.781, 1.010]; ground truth is the 44 items where both agreed
and neither said UNJUDGEABLE.

    tier                            precision                  recall
    falsifier_identity              n=0 -- never fires cross-model on real data
    same_computed_outcome           13/13 = 100.0% [77.2, 100.0]   54.2%
    signature_merge                 8/17  =  47.1% [26.2,  69.0]   33.3%
    both together (as first built)  21/30 =  70.0% [52.1,  83.3]   87.5%

    base rate among same-location cross-model pairs: 3/14 = 21.4% [7.6, 47.6]

READ THAT TABLE BEFORE QUOTING ANY NUMBER FROM THIS TOOL. The signature tier is
47% precise -- better than the 21% base rate and not much else. The
computed-outcome tier did not make a single false link in 13 adjudicated cases,
which is exactly what `computed_outcomes` was built for ("it can answer where
tier 2 is weakest"). So the HEADLINE co-discovery rate is the outcome tier
alone, and the signature tier is reported beside it as CANDIDATES, never folded
in silently. n=13 is small: 100% carries a lower bound of 77.2%, not certainty.

USAGE
-----
    python3 scripts/codiscovery_link.py                 # real archive, summary
    python3 scripts/codiscovery_link.py --json out.json # full edge set
    python3 scripts/codiscovery_link.py --controls      # + null / same-model controls
    python3 scripts/codiscovery_link.py --include-sim   # do not filter simulated runs
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import collections
import glob
import hashlib
import json
import math
import pathlib
import random
import re
import sys
from typing import Dict, FrozenSet, List, Optional, Sequence, Tuple

REPO = pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO))
sys.path.insert(0, str(REPO / "bench"))

from bench.convergence_location import (  # noqa: E402
    WITHIN_LOCATION_THRESHOLD,
    _gate_population,
    computed_outcomes,
    finding_locations,
    outcome_agreement,
    signature_similarity,
    stem_signature,
    target_symbols,
)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provenance. Copied in BEHAVIOUR, not in code, from
# scripts/latent_control_audit._is_simulated (0ec926d): PARSE FIRST, CLASSIFY
# SECOND. A real run that QUOTES the -SIM marker must not be excluded from its
# own evidence base.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def is_simulated(doc: dict, fp: pathlib.Path) -> bool:
    sa = doc.get("severity_admissibility")
    if isinstance(sa, dict) and sa.get("severity_provenance") == "simulated":
        return True
    if doc.get("_simulated"):
        return True
    models = doc.get("models") or doc.get("model_labels") or []
    if isinstance(models, (list, tuple)) and any(
            isinstance(m, str) and m.upper().endswith("-SIM") for m in models):
        return True
    name = fp.parent.name.lower()
    return name.startswith("sim") or "_sim" in name or "simulated" in name


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHICH ENTRIES ARE FINDINGS AT ALL.
#
# THIS IS THE CORRECTION THAT MATTERS, and I nearly shipped without it. The
# first run of this linker returned 56.10% co-discovery over every registry
# entry. Sampling eight linked pairs at random -- which is the step that is
# always skipped -- showed that several were not two discoveries at all: they
# were two models' PROTOCOL CHATTER. `WITHDRAW ChatGPT_F010 -- schema marked it
# DUPLICATE`, `VERDICT: CHALLENGE C0031`, `SCHEMA_FEEDBACK_RESPONSE:` and
# truncated `FINDING_ID:` fragments are registered as canonical findings and
# then link to each other, because two verdict messages about one finding share
# every identifier in it.
#
# MEASURED: 163 of 2267 real archived entries (7.2%) open with a protocol token,
# but they account for at least one endpoint of 895 of 2556 strong cross-model
# edges -- 35.0%, Wilson [33.2%, 36.9%]. Chatter is 7% of the population and 35%
# of the evidence, because chatter clusters with chatter.
#
# So the number is reported over three populations and the caller chooses:
#
#   all      every registry entry. Comparable to the founder's 2/2050 denominator
#            and to nothing else. Contaminated; kept only so the before/after
#            comparison is like for like.
#   gate     `_gate_population` -- what the convergence gate actually sees.
#            The project's own answer to "which entries count".
#   critical gate population at severity >= 0.7. The population gamma reads and
#            the only one a saturation curve should ever be built on.
#
# The chatter filter is applied to `gate` and `critical` and is reported
# separately, never silently.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#: Registry entries whose text is panel PROTOCOL rather than a discovery. Kept
#: deliberately narrow and anchored near the start of the text: a real finding
#: that merely MENTIONS a verdict must not be filtered out. Every pattern here
#: was read off an actual archived entry.
_PROTOCOL = re.compile(
    r"(?im)^\s*(?:-\s*)?(?:VERDICT\s*:|CONFIRM\s+C\d|CHALLENGE\s+C\d"
    r"|WITHDRAW\s|MERGE\s+C\d|SCHEMA_FEEDBACK|NO\s+NEW\s+(?:CONFIRM|FINDING))")


def is_protocol_chatter(description: str) -> bool:
    """True when this entry is a panel message, not a discovery.

    Anchored to the first 400 characters, because a finding that quotes a
    verdict later in its body is still a finding.
    """
    return bool(_PROTOCOL.search((description or "")[:400]))


def select_population(entries: dict, mode: str, *,
                      critical_threshold: float = 0.7) -> list:
    """The entries to link, under one of the three named populations."""
    vals = list(entries.values())
    if mode == "all":
        return vals
    keep = _gate_population(entries)
    keep = [e for e in keep if not is_protocol_chatter(e.get("description"))]
    if mode == "critical":
        keep = [e for e in keep
                if float(e.get("severity") or 0.0) >= critical_threshold]
    return keep


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The PAIRWISE form of the rule the runner already runs.
#
# identity_decision is STREAMING: it folds a new finding against everything seen
# so far and answers new / not-new. A co-discovery GRAPH needs the pairwise
# form. Every tier below dispatches to the same primitive at the same constant,
# so this is one rule in two shapes, not two rules -- pinned by
# test_codiscovery_link_2026-09-02.py, which fails if WITHIN_LOCATION_THRESHOLD
# moves and this does not move with it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

#: Tiers, strongest evidence first. The deciding tier is recorded on every edge:
#: an edge whose provenance is unknown cannot be audited, and a reader must be
#: able to discount the weak tier without re-running anything.
TIER_FALSIFIER = "falsifier_identity"        # tier 0 -- decisive when it fires
TIER_OUTCOME = "same_computed_outcome"       # tier 1 -- same asserted quantity
TIER_SIGNATURE = "signature_merge"           # tier 2 -- Jaccard over STEM signature
TIER_NO_WITNESS = "no_distinctness_witness"  # tier 3 -- WEAK, reported apart

STRONG_TIERS = (TIER_FALSIFIER, TIER_OUTCOME, TIER_SIGNATURE)


class Item:
    """One archived registry entry, with the identity rule's inputs precomputed."""

    __slots__ = ("cid", "seat", "model", "severity", "status", "locations",
                 "signature", "outcomes", "falsifier")

    def __init__(self, entry: dict, symbols: FrozenSet[str],
                 identity: Dict[str, str]):
        desc = entry.get("description") or ""
        self.cid = entry.get("canonical_id") or ""
        self.seat = entry.get("source_model") or ""
        # SEATS ARE NOT MODELS (registry.model_identity, 2026-09-01): in the live
        # config Codex and ChatGPT declare the SAME underlying model_id, so a
        # tally of distinct SEAT labels counts one model twice. Co-discovery is a
        # claim about MODELS. An unknown seat resolves to itself, exactly as the
        # runner's map does, so archived data behaves as before.
        self.model = identity.get(self.seat, self.seat)
        self.severity = float(entry.get("severity") or 0.0)
        self.status = entry.get("status") or ""
        self.locations = finding_locations(desc, symbols)
        self.signature = stem_signature(desc)
        self.outcomes = computed_outcomes(desc)
        body = (entry.get("falsifier_code") or "").strip()
        self.falsifier = (hashlib.sha256(body.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()
                          if body else None)


def same_defect(a: Item, b: Item, *,
                threshold: float = WITHIN_LOCATION_THRESHOLD) -> Optional[str]:
    """Do these two findings describe ONE defect? Returns the deciding tier or None.

    Mirrors identity_decision's "not new" branches, in its own order:

      tier 0  identical falsifier body   -> the project's own criterion, inverted
                                            from 976b6b5. Decisive when it fires.
      (gate)  a shared LOCATION, or both unlocated. _priors_for refuses to compare
              a located finding against an unlocated one, and so does this: it is
              the guard that stopped a signature match merging what location
              keying had counted (Exp 48/49).
      tier 1  the same computed outcome  -> outcome_agreement == "SAME"
      tier 2  signature Jaccard >= 0.20  -> the calibrated within-location rule
      tier 3  one side carries no signature -> no_distinctness_witness. In the
              GATE that direction is conservative (withhold a novelty count). As
              a co-discovery EDGE it is the opposite: it asserts a link from an
              absence of evidence. Returned, flagged, excluded from the headline.
    """
    if a.falsifier is not None and a.falsifier == b.falsifier:
        return TIER_FALSIFIER
    if a.locations or b.locations:
        if not (a.locations & b.locations):
            return None
    if outcome_agreement(a.outcomes, b.outcomes) == "SAME":
        return TIER_OUTCOME
    if signature_similarity(a.signature, b.signature) >= threshold:
        return TIER_SIGNATURE
    if (a.locations & b.locations) and (not a.signature or not b.signature):
        return TIER_NO_WITNESS
    return None


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Statistics. Wilson, because n is small and Wald lies at the edges.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def wilson(k: int, n: int, z: float = 1.959963984540054) -> Tuple[float, float]:
    if n == 0:
        return (0.0, 0.0)
    p = k / n
    d = 1 + z * z / n
    c = p + z * z / (2 * n)
    h = z * math.sqrt((p * (1 - p) + z * z / (4 * n)) / n)
    return ((c - h) / d, (c + h) / d)


def pct(k: int, n: int) -> str:
    if n == 0:
        return "n=0"
    lo, hi = wilson(k, n)
    return f"{k}/{n} = {100 * k / n:.2f}% [{100 * lo:.2f}%, {100 * hi:.2f}%]"


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Loading
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def _symbols_for(doc: dict) -> FrozenSet[str]:
    """Target symbols, from the target file when it is still on disk.

    Falls back to the union of backticked identifiers across the run's own
    descriptions. That fallback is deliberately GENEROUS on the location gate --
    more symbols means more findings carry a location, which makes the gate bind
    MORE pairs, not fewer -- so a missing target file cannot silently disable the
    location guard and inflate the count through the unlocated branch.
    """
    tf = doc.get("target_file") or ""
    for cand in (REPO / tf, pathlib.Path(tf)):
        try:
            if cand.is_file():
                return target_symbols(str(cand))
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            pass
    syms = set()
    for e in ((doc.get("registry") or {}).get("entries") or {}).values():
        syms |= set(re.findall(r"`([A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_\.]{4,})`",
                               e.get("description") or ""))
    return frozenset(syms)


def load_runs(include_sim: bool = False) -> List[dict]:
    runs = []
    seen = set()
    for path in sorted(glob.glob(
            str(REPO / "bench" / "logs" / "**" / "*report*.json"), recursive=True)):
        fp = pathlib.Path(path)
        try:
            doc = json.loads(fp.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="ignore"))
        except Exception:                                   # noqa: BLE001
            continue
        if not isinstance(doc, dict):
            continue
        entries = (doc.get("registry") or {}).get("entries")
        if not isinstance(entries, dict) or not entries:
            continue
        sim = is_simulated(doc, fp)
        if sim and not include_sim:
            continue
        # The archive holds byte-duplicate reports written twice under different
        # directory stamps (exp36_evidence_latest; every sim45 pair). Counting
        # both doubles every rate. Fingerprint on (experiment, start, size).
        key = (doc.get("experiment"), doc.get("start_time"), len(entries))
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        symbols = _symbols_for(doc)
        identity = ((doc.get("registry") or {}).get("model_identity")
                    or doc.get("model_identity") or {})
        if not isinstance(identity, dict):
            identity = {}
        runs.append({
            "path": str(fp.relative_to(REPO)), "name": fp.parent.name,
            "start": doc.get("start_time") or "", "simulated": sim,
            "symbols": symbols,
            "entries": entries,
            "identity": identity,
            "items": [Item(e, symbols, identity) for e in entries.values()],
            "recorded_codiscovery": sum(
                1 for e in entries.values() if e.get("codiscovery")),
        })
    return runs


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The linker
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def link_run(items: Sequence[Item], *, cross_model_only: bool = True) -> List[dict]:
    edges = []
    for i in range(len(items)):
        for j in range(i + 1, len(items)):
            a, b = items[i], items[j]
            if cross_model_only and a.model == b.model:
                continue
            tier = same_defect(a, b)
            if tier:
                edges.append({"a": a.cid, "b": b.cid, "a_model": a.model,
                              "b_model": b.model, "tier": tier})
    return edges


def components(items, edges) -> Dict[str, str]:
    """Union-find. A CO-DISCOVERED defect is a component drawn from >=2 models."""
    parent = {it.cid: it.cid for it in items}

    def find(x):
        while parent[x] != x:
            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]
            x = parent[x]
        return x

    for e in edges:
        ra, rb = find(e["a"]), find(e["b"])
        if ra != rb:
            parent[ra] = rb
    return {cid: find(cid) for cid in parent}


def analyse(runs: List[dict], *, strong_only: bool = True,
            population: str = "all") -> dict:
    per_run, tiers = [], collections.Counter()
    tot_f = tot_l = tot_b = tot_o = 0
    for r in runs:
        sel = select_population(r["entries"], population)
        items = [Item(e, r["symbols"], r["identity"]) for e in sel]
        r["_sel_items"] = items
        r["_sel_recorded"] = sum(1 for e in sel if e.get("codiscovery"))
        edges = link_run(items)
        tiers.update(e["tier"] for e in edges)
        use = [e for e in edges if e["tier"] in STRONG_TIERS] if strong_only else edges
        oc = set()
        for e in edges:
            if e["tier"] in (TIER_OUTCOME, TIER_FALSIFIER):
                oc |= {e["a"], e["b"]}
        comp = components(items, use)
        groups = collections.defaultdict(list)
        for it in items:
            groups[comp[it.cid]].append(it)
        multi = [g for g in groups.values() if len({m.model for m in g}) > 1]
        linked = sum(len(g) for g in multi)
        per_run.append({
            "run": r["name"], "start": r["start"], "simulated": r["simulated"],
            "findings": len(items), "distinct_models": len({i.model for i in items}),
            "recorded_codiscovery": r["_sel_recorded"],
            "cross_model_edges": len(use), "codiscovered_findings": linked,
            "codiscovery_groups": len(multi),
            "distinct_defects_after": len(groups),
        })
        per_run[-1]["codiscovered_outcome_tier"] = len(oc)
        tot_f += len(items)
        tot_l += linked
        tot_o += len(oc)
        tot_b += r["_sel_recorded"]
    return {"runs": per_run, "tier_counts": dict(tiers),
            "totals": {"runs": len(runs), "findings": tot_f,
                       "codiscovery_before": tot_b,
                       "codiscovery_outcome_only": tot_o,
                       "codiscovery_after": tot_l}}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CONTROLS. A linker with no false-positive floor can report any number it
# likes. Both controls are executed, not argued.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


def null_control(runs: List[dict], limit: int = 200000) -> dict:
    """Findings from runs against DIFFERENT targets cannot be co-discovery.

    The link rate here is the rule's false-positive floor. Scored LIKE FOR LIKE:
    each item keeps the locations computed against its OWN run's symbol set,
    exactly as in the live comparison, so the control measures the rule and not
    a vocabulary mismatch.
    """
    rng = random.Random(20260902)
    pool = [(ri, it) for ri, r in enumerate(runs)
            for it in r.get("_sel_items", r["items"])]
    hits, n, tries = collections.Counter(), 0, 0
    while n < limit and tries < limit * 10:
        tries += 1
        (ri, a), (rj, b) = rng.choice(pool), rng.choice(pool)
        if ri == rj or a.model == b.model:
            continue
        n += 1
        tier = same_defect(a, b)
        if tier:
            hits[tier] += 1
    strong = sum(v for k, v in hits.items() if k in STRONG_TIERS)
    return {"pairs": n, "strong_hits": strong, "by_tier": dict(hits),
            "wilson": wilson(strong, n)}


def same_model_control(runs: List[dict]) -> dict:
    """SAME-model links: duplicates, not co-discovery.

    A model re-raising its own defect across rounds is the population this rule
    was calibrated on, so it is the closest thing to a positive control the
    archive offers. If the cross-model rate were far BELOW it, the rule would be
    failing across models specifically.
    """
    linked = tot = 0
    for r in runs:
        items = r.get("_sel_items", r["items"])
        edges = [e for e in
                 [{"a": items[i].cid, "b": items[j].cid,
                   "tier": same_defect(items[i], items[j])}
                  for i in range(len(items)) for j in range(i + 1, len(items))
                  if items[i].model == items[j].model]
                 if e["tier"] in STRONG_TIERS]
        comp = components(items, edges)
        g = collections.defaultdict(list)
        for it in items:
            g[comp[it.cid]].append(it)
        linked += sum(len(v) for v in g.values() if len(v) > 1)
        tot += len(items)
    return {"linked": linked, "findings": tot}


def main(argv=None) -> int:
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description="cross-model co-discovery linker")
    ap.add_argument("--json")
    ap.add_argument("--controls", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--include-sim", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--with-weak", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--population", choices=("all", "gate", "critical"),
                    default="all",
                    help="which registry entries count as findings; see the "
                         "block above select_population")
    args = ap.parse_args(argv)

    runs = load_runs(include_sim=args.include_sim)
    res = analyse(runs, strong_only=not args.with_weak,
                  population=args.population)
    t = res["totals"]
    print(f"runs={t['runs']}  findings={t['findings']}  "
          f"population={args.population}  "
          f"({'real+simulated' if args.include_sim else 'real only'})\n")
    print("CO-DISCOVERY RATE")
    print(f"  before (field as the runner wrote it): "
          f"{pct(t['codiscovery_before'], t['findings'])}")
    print(f"  after  HEADLINE, outcome tier only   : "
          f"{pct(t['codiscovery_outcome_only'], t['findings'])}")
    print(f"         precision 13/13 = 100.0% [77.2%, 100.0%], recall 54.2% "
          f"(blind 2-reader, kappa 0.896)")
    print(f"  after  + signature CANDIDATES        : "
          f"{pct(t['codiscovery_after'], t['findings'])}")
    print(f"         precision 21/30 = 70.0% [52.1%, 83.3%] -- do not quote as "
          f"the rate\n")
    print("  edges by deciding tier:")
    for k, v in sorted(res["tier_counts"].items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]):
        print(f"    {k:26} {v}" + ("" if k in STRONG_TIERS
                                   else "   <- WEAK, excluded above"))
    print()
    print(f"{'run':52} {'n':>4} {'mdl':>4} {'before':>7} {'after':>6} {'grp':>4}")
    for r in res["runs"]:
        print(f"{r['run'][:52]:52} {r['findings']:4d} {r['distinct_models']:4d} "
              f"{r['recorded_codiscovery']:7d} {r['codiscovered_findings']:6d} "
              f"{r['codiscovery_groups']:4d}")
    if args.controls:
        print("\nCONTROLS")
        nc = null_control(runs)
        print("  null (cross-run, different target, different model)  <- FP floor")
        print(f"    {pct(nc['strong_hits'], nc['pairs'])}")
        print(f"    by tier: {nc['by_tier']}")
        sm = same_model_control(runs)
        print(f"  same-model (duplicates, positive control): "
              f"{pct(sm['linked'], sm['findings'])}")
    if args.json:
        pathlib.Path(args.json).write_text(json.dumps(
            {"summary": res,
             "edges": {r["name"]: link_run(r.get("_sel_items", r["items"]))
                       for r in runs}},
            indent=2), encoding="utf-8")
        print(f"\nwrote {args.json}")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())

