"""Q2: designs (a) declare-and-caveat and (b) declare-and-reallocate, RUN, not argued.

Drives the real `bench/routing.py` ladder under two panel compositions and two
designs. The model dispatch is a deterministic stub parameterised by one number,
so every figure reproduces offline with no vendor call.

WHAT THIS ADDS TO THE MECHANISM ANALYSIS
----------------------------------------
The mechanism argument says design (a)'s second rung is worthless on a
single-model panel because "rho = 1 by construction -- identical weights,
identical instruction condition, identical prompt". THAT STEP IS WRONG, and it
is the step the whole comparison rests on.

Vendor endpoints are sampled, not evaluated. Two calls to one model on one
prompt are two draws, not one repeated answer, so rho < 1 even under design (a).
This script therefore does NOT assume a value: it SWEEPS rho and reports where
the two designs actually separate, which is the honest form of the answer while
the number is unmeasured.

WHAT IS ASSUMED, STATED PLAINLY
-------------------------------
rho_a (same model, same instruction condition, two draws) and rho_b (same model,
two DIFFERENT instruction conditions) are both UNMEASURED in this archive. The
0C.29 contrast that would measure them lapsed on 2026-04-02. Nothing below
should be quoted as a measurement of either; the deliverable is the SHAPE of the
difference and the size of the experiment needed to resolve it.

CORPUS FACT THAT BOUNDS THIS WHOLE QUESTION, measured 2026-09-02
-----------------------------------------------------------------
ZERO of the 29 real archived runs was effectively single-model. All 29 ran five
distinct vendors. The nine "six seats, one model" runs are the `sim45_*`
SIMULATED rehearsals, and `model_identity` is absent from every archived report.
So design (b) targets a mode that has never once been exercised for real, and no
archived number can validate it. That is an argument for building (b) behind an
honest declaration, not for trusting a projection of it.
